<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Guru Nanak</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1469">1469</a>–<a href="/years/1539">1539</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Nanak, Baba Nanak</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Guru Nanak was the founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhism</a> and the first of its ten gurus, born near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> in 1469. After a transforming religious experience he travelled widely, by tradition as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, teaching devotion to one formless God, the equality of all people regardless of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a>, and honest work and shared food. He settled at Kartarpur, where a community of disciples, the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhs">Sikhs</a>, gathered around him; his hymns open the Guru Granth Sahib, Sikhism's scripture.</p></div>
